    "q-106811": {
      "dom_id": "step-q106811",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "Let $F$ be the set of all functions $f:(0,\\infty )\\to R$ of the form $f(x)=\\frac{a}{x},$ where $a\\in R.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $g(x)\\in F,$ where $g(x)=\\frac{1}{2x}.$\n\nII. For any $f(x)\\in F$ and $k\\in R,$ we have $k\\cdot f(x)\\in F.$\n\nIII. The set is closed under scalar multiplication.",
      "worked_solution": "Let's examine each of our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The set $F$ contains only functions of the form $\\frac{a}{x},$ where $a$ is a real number. The function $g(x)=\\frac{1}{2x}$ is a function of this form with $a=\\frac{1}{2}.$ So, $g(x)\\in F.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, suppose $f(x)=\\frac{a}{x}\\in F,$ where $a\\in R,$ and $k\\in R.$ Then,\n\n$$\nk\\cdot f(x)=k\\cdot (\\frac{a}{x})=\\frac{ka}{x}.\n$$\n\nSince $(ka)$ is a real number, we conclude that $\\frac{ka}{x}\\in F,$ i.e., $k\\cdot f(x)\\in F.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, the set is closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
