    "q-94735": {
      "dom_id": "step-q94735",
      "name": "Question 12",
      "problem": "Let $E$ be the set of all functions of the form $f(x)=a\\,\\text{ }{2}^{x},$ where $a\\in R.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. For any $f(x)\\in E,$ we have $1\\cdot f(x)=f(x).$\n\nII. For any $f(x)\\in E$ and $\\alpha ,\\beta \\in R$, we have $(\\alpha +\\beta )f(x)=\\alpha f(x)+\\beta f(x).$\n\nIII. For any $f(x),g(x)\\in E$ and $\\alpha \\in R$, we have $\\alpha (f(x)+g(x))=\\alpha f(x)+\\alpha g(x).$",
      "worked_solution": "Let's examine each of our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, for any $f(x)\\in E$ and for any fixed $x\\in R,$ the result of $f(x)$ is just a real number. Thus,\n\n$$\n(1\\cdot f)(x)=1\\cdot f(x)=f(x).\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, for any $f(x)\\in E$ and for any fixed $x\\in R,$ the result of $f(x)$ is just a real number. Thus, for any $\\alpha ,\\beta \\in R,$ we obtain that\n\n$$\n(\\alpha +\\beta )f(x)=\\alpha f(x)+\\beta f(x).\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, for any $f(x)\\in E$ and for any fixed $x\\in R,$ the results of $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ are just real numbers. Thus, for any $\\alpha \\in R,$ we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\alpha (f(x)+g(x))=\\alpha f(x)+\\alpha g(x).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
