    "q-106810": {
      "dom_id": "step-q106810",
      "name": "Question 13",
      "problem": "Let $J$ be the set of all polynomials with real coefficients and whose degree is at most $2.$  Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\frac{1}{x}\\in J$\n\nII. For any $p(x)\\in J,$ we have $1\\cdot p(x)\\in J$\n\nIII. For any $p(x),q(x)\\in J$ and $\\alpha \\in R,$ we have $\\alpha (p(x)-q(x))=\\alpha p(x)-\\alpha q(x)$",
      "worked_solution": "Let's examine each of our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. The set $J$ contains only polynomials whose degree is at most $2.$ However, $\\frac{1}{x}$ is *not* a polynomial. So, $\\frac{1}{x}\\notin J.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, for any $p(x)\\in J$ and for any fixed $x\\in R,$ the result of $p(x)$ is just a real number. Thus,\n\n$$\n1\\cdot p(x)=p(x).\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, for any $p(x),q(x)\\in J,$ and for any fixed $x\\in R,$ the results of $p(x)$ and $q(x)$ are just real numbers. Thus, for any $\\alpha \\in R,$ we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\alpha (p(x)-q(x))=\\alpha p(x)-\\alpha q(x).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
